N.B. if any one device is compromised, criminals may be able to access all data through cloud storage service providers, e.g. iCloud, Google Drive.

#### Wireless Network Security at Home

Wireless networks allow Wi-Fi enabled devices, such as laptops and tablets, to connect to the network by way of a preset network identifier, known as the service set identifier (SSID). Although a wireless router can be configured so that it doesn’t broadcast the SSID, this should not be considered adequate security for a wireless network.

Hackers will be aware of the preset SSID and default password. Therefore, these details should be changed to prevent intruders from entering your home wireless network. Furthermore, you should encrypt wireless communication by enabling wireless security and the WPA2 encryption feature on your wireless router. But be aware, even with WPA2 encryption enabled, a wireless network can still be vulnerable.

#### KRACK (Key Reinstallation Attack)

A type of attack that targets the WPA2 protocol, which is used to secure Wi-Fi networks. It allows an attacker to intercept and manipulate the communication between a device and a Wi-Fi access point, potentially allowing them to steal sensitive information such as passwords and credit card numbers. The flaw affects all modern,protected WiFi systems. To mitigate the risk of KRACK attacks:

- keep devices and software up to date with the latest security patches and updates.
- use a virtual private network (VPN) to help protect data when using public Wi-Fi networks.
- use wired connections for any wired (NIC) interface as they are generally more secure than wireless connections.

#### Public Wi-Fi Risks

accessing online services via public wireless networks or Wi-Fi hotspots comes with risks. These risks mean that it is best not to access or send any personal information when using public Wi-Fi.

Always verify that devices aren’t configured with file and media sharing and require user authentication with encryption.

Use an encrypted VPN service to prevent others from intercepting information (known as ‘eavesdropping’) over a public wireless network. This service provides secure Internet access by encrypting the connection between device and VPN server. Even if hackers intercept a data transmission in an encrypted VPN tunnel, they will not be able to decipher it.

#### Using a Passphrase

To prevent unauthorized device access, use passphrases instead of passwords. A passphrase generally takes the form of a sentence (‘Acat th@tlov3sd0gs.’) and it’s longer than a typical password - so less vulnerable to dictionary or brute-force attacks.

#### Password Guidelines

The United States National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) has published improved password requirements. NIST standards are intended for government applications but can serve as a standard for other sectors. The NIST password guidelines recommend the following:

- Use a minimum of 8 characters and a maximum of 64 characters.
- Use a combination of upper and lower case letters, numbers, and special characters.
- Avoid using common words, phrases, or easily guessable information (e.g., birthdays, names, or simple patterns).
- Do not reuse passwords across different accounts or services.
- Consider using a password manager to generate and store complex passwords securely.
- Change passwords regularly, especially if there is any suspicion that an account may have been compromised.
- Enable multi-factor authentication (MFA) whenever possible to add an extra layer of security.
- Be cautious when using public Wi-Fi and avoid accessing sensitive information when connected to these networks.

### Data Maintenance

#### What Is Encryption?

Encryption is the process of converting information into a form in which unauthorized parties cannot read it. Only a trusted, authorized person with the secret key or password can decrypt the data and access it in its original form.

N.B. encryption itself does not prevent someone from intercepting the data. It can only prevent an unauthorized person from viewing or accessing the content. Some criminals may decide to simply encrypt your data and make it unusable until you pay a ransom.

#### How To Encrypt Data?

Software programs are used to encrypt files, folders and even entire drives.

Encrypting File System (EFS) is a Windows feature that can encrypt data. It is directly linked to a specific user account and only the user that encrypts the data will be able to access it after it has been encrypted using EFS.

